A||||7||fiero56||my final list for 2.8 build, green light to go?||||1||
Z||000000||fiero56||08-19-2001||12:03 AM||||The time is finally here for me to get going with this. I feel I have lerned enough information to make informed decisions about everything I am buying for this. I plan to be placing orders this coming week.<P>I am using a 2.8 block bored .040".<P>Piston:<BR>part# 1463<BR>silv-o-lite brand<BR>8.5:1 c/r(destroked for typical block amount removed during machining)<P>Piston rings:<BR>part#- E411x<BR>Sealed Power<P>cam:<BR>part# 16-232-4; grind 252H<BR>CompCams brand; High Energy<BR>Complete cam kit with cam, lifters, springs, retainers, locks, seals, timing chain set<P>Rockers:<BR>part# 1413-12<BR>CompComs roller rocker<P>pushrods:<BR>part# 7816-12<BR>CompCams<P>valves:<BR>stainless steel by SI<P>oil pump:<BR>part# m-95hv<BR>melling high-volume<P>Factory replacement head and main bolts<BR>ARP rod bolts<BR>All bearings are Clevite77<BR>Gasket set will be ROL or Fel-Pro<P>Heads are receiving a 3-angle valve job, and exhaust manifolds are receiving typical restriction removal.<BR>Engine will be using typical neutrally balanced crank, and will be attached to a new balancer.<P>Anything I am forgetting. I know I must have left a few things out, its a little late, and I'm not thinking really clear.<P>I still need to find 87-88 timing cover and oil pan. Shaun, I will be sending you an e-mail soon about ordering that crank from you.<P>I have externally balanced crank and early style timing cover and oil pan to get rid of sometime.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Steve Normington||08-19-2001||12:45 AM||||Not to complicate things, but if you are going to be getting a new crank, are you getting the 3.1 crank? Are you going to gasket match the intake manifold? Also, you will need a new flywheel/flexplate. If you are going to a 3.1, you should think about new injectors and a adjustable fuel pressure regulator.<p>[This message has been edited by Steve Normington (edited 08-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||08-19-2001||08:38 AM||||Nope,<P>I am sticking with the 2.8 crank. I am just getting rid of the externally balanced crank and putting in a neutrally balanced crank. I've already got 2 neutrally balanced flywheels. One is original from my Formula, and one with about 15K miles.<P>Might as well add, I will be connecting the tranny through a carquest clutch set. I just need to find one first.||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||08-19-2001||09:44 AM||||What is the Machinist doing for machine work?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||08-19-2001||09:44 AM||||all looks good, except the 8.5:1 pistons, why not go for a higher ratio after the machineing?  As high as 9.5:1 or even 10:1 final compression ought to work fine, and will produce a more efficent (read: powerful) and revvy engine.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000005||fiero56||08-19-2001||12:38 PM||||I thought about higher compression, but am looking for long term reliability, especially in the bottom end. While speaking with CompCams I asked about C/Rs, and the highest they recommend for my chosen cam is 9:1, due to valve-timing, duration etc. An added plus is a lower c/r leaves open the possibility for a turbo in a few years <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P>I am still speaking with UEM about C/Rs and pistons. They have a unit that is not destroked, but I need to supply head cc, and I haven't been able to find it here yet, but know that it was posted before. Still searching.<P>Block will be having the decks shaved, rods will be recondidtioned, crank 'fluxed and machined, etc, etc. I don't have the list in front of me with all work to be done, but the machinest refused to do work on the block unless I had certain operations performed(which I would have had done anyway, but he emphasized they were also requirements). He seems to really know his stuff, so I'm not worried. He better be good, he's making some big $$$ off of me.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||08-19-2001||03:20 PM||||Get the whole rotating assembly balanced.  Not only will the engine feel smoother and rev faster, it will last longer.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">        <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed<P><FONT SIZE="1">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<BR><FONT COLOR="red">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
